# DeepSee — Project Rules

**Read this before touching anything. It is the project's memory.**

DeepSee is a multi-tenant Windows device operations platform: a web Command Center
plus a Windows Agent plus a relay, letting individuals, businesses, schools,
enterprises and MSPs enroll Windows PCs once and then remotely access, monitor,
manage, automate and support them.

The authoritative product specification is `docs/PRODUCT.md`. Its `§n` section
numbers are the citation format used in code comments, commit messages and pull
requests. **Every non-trivial change cites the section it implements.** If you
cannot name the section, you are probably adding something the product does not
need, or you have not read the spec.

`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` records what is actually built and how it maps to the spec.

---

## 1. Never drift from the spec

- Before implementing, find the supporting section in `docs/PRODUCT.md`.
- If the spec is ambiguous, resolve it in the spec first, then implement. Do not
  let code become the only expression of a decision.
- If reality forces a deviation, note the deviation and its reason in a code
  comment AND update `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`. An undocumented deviation is a bug.

## 2. Hard rules (violating these is never acceptable)

1. **Tenant isolation is enforced server-side.** `org_id` on writes always comes
   from a server-derived value, never from client input. RLS policies use BOTH
   `USING` and `WITH CHECK`. (§8, §86, §215)
   - A `FOR ALL` policy with only `USING` does not filter INSERT. That is a
     security hole, not a style preference.
   - Never read a tenant from `user_metadata`: end users can write it. Only
     `app_metadata` is server-controlled.

2. **No silent success.** Every operation resolves to one of: `pending`, `running`,
   `success`, `failed`, `timed_out`, `cancelled`, `device_offline`,
   `permission_denied`. Never render a success state you did not observe. Never let
   a UI show "connecting" forever. (§124, §229)

3. **No mock data in shipped UI.** If a feature is not built, the UI says so
   plainly. Hard-coded device lists, fabricated metrics and simulated streams are
   forbidden — they make the product lie about itself.

4. **No OS-security bypass and no covert capture.** Do not implement or add
   dependencies that: crack passwords, bypass Windows authentication or encryption,
   defeat activation, activate camera/microphone without consent, suppress
   operating-system privacy indicators, or conceal from the person at the machine
   that they are monitored or controlled. (§160, §237)
   - Camera/microphone requires a tenant flag AND a per-device grant AND a consent
     record AND an auditable session.
   - Never claim to read encrypted HTTPS page contents just because a device is
     enrolled. Metadata telemetry is not content. (§50)

5. **Secrets have no fallbacks.** A required credential that is missing must fail
   loudly at startup. Never commit a real secret; never write a placeholder that
   looks real. (§214)

6. **Session mode is enforced server-side.** Hiding a button is not authorization.
   View-only means the server refuses injected input. (§17, §212)

7. **The agent validates every command** for tenant, device, expiry, permission and
   uniqueness before executing it. (§210, §211)

## 3. Working agreements

- **Verify, never assume.** Running the command is the only evidence. Report what
  the tool output actually said. If something could not be verified (missing
  runtime, no Docker, no credentials), say so explicitly rather than implying
  success.
- **Small, coherent, reviewable changes.** One concern per change.
- **Fix root causes.** If a lint rule or type error appears, fix the code. Do not
  disable the rule, widen a type to `any`, or add a skip — unless you can justify
  it in a comment and the justification is not "it was easier".
- **Never use `any`** where a real type can be expressed. If the shape is genuinely
  unknown, use `unknown` and narrow. `any` deletes type safety exactly where bugs
  are most expensive.
- **Comment the why, not the what.** Explain constraints, invariants and surprises.
  Do not narrate the code.
- **Prefer deleting to accumulating.** Dead code, unused exports and
  commented-out blocks get removed.

## 4. Verification gates

A change is not done until all of these pass locally:

```
pnpm type-check     # tsc --noEmit across the workspace
pnpm lint           # eslint across the workspace packages
pnpm test           # unit tests
pnpm build          # production build of web + relay
pnpm db:migrate     # applies to an empty database, twice, cleanly
pnpm db:test        # tenant-isolation and RLS invariants
```

Plus, for any change that touches a user-visible flow, a manual walkthrough of that
flow in a browser.

Do not report a task complete while any of these fail, or while any of them was not
actually run.

## 5. Environment facts (this machine)

| Thing | State |
|---|---|
| Node | v24.x |
| pnpm | 12.8.1 — pinned in `package.json` `packageManager`, in CI, and in the web Dockerfile. Keep all three in sync. |
| PostgreSQL | 18.3, running as service `postgresql-x64-18` on `:5432`, `scram-sha-256` auth |
| Docker | **not installed** — the compose stack cannot be run or verified here |
| .NET SDK | **not installed** — `apps/agent` cannot be compiled or tested here |
| Windows symlinks | **not permitted** (no Developer Mode, not elevated) — which is why `next.config.ts` disables `output: 'standalone'` on win32 |

## 6. Layout

```
apps/web           Next.js 15 Command Center (App Router, RSC + client islands)
apps/relay         Node WebSocket relay (device + admin sockets, control-plane fan-out)
apps/agent         C# .NET 8 Windows Agent: CoreService (Session 0) + Worker (user session)
packages/database  SQL migrations, migration runner, seeds, isolation tests
packages/shared    Cross-cutting TypeScript types, constants, utilities
packages/ui        Shared presentational components
docs/              PRODUCT.md (spec), ARCHITECTURE.md (as-built), DEVELOPMENT, DEPLOYMENT
```

## 7. UI rules

- **No icon fonts, no emoji, no star glyphs.** Device state is conveyed by the
  geometric `.dot-*` classes plus text labels. This keeps the console legible in
  high-contrast mode and to screen readers.
- Use semantic tokens (`bg-surface`, `text-text-secondary`, `bg-status-online`) from
  `apps/web/tailwind.config.ts`. Never hard-code hex values or reach for raw
  Tailwind palette colours such as `slate-600` inside components.
- The console is a dense operational tool, not a marketing page. Prefer tabular
  density, monospaced numerics and restrained motion.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Keep visible focus rings on every interactive
  element.
- Do not darken `text-muted` below its current value; it was contrast-checked
  against WCAG AA. Re-measure before changing any colour pair.

## 8. Current state (keep this honest)

Phase 0 (baseline hygiene) is complete: git initialised, type-check/lint/build all
green, Dockerfiles fixed, CI and deploy pipelines rewritten, environment layer typed
and validated, docs committed.

Phases 1–14 in `docs/PRODUCT.md` §15 are **not built**. Command Center pages are
still static mockups and the agent cannot yet talk to the platform. The next work is
Phase 1: schema v2, idempotent tracked migrations, correct RLS with `WITH CHECK`,
and an automated tenant-isolation test.

When you complete a phase, update this section.